  "title": "several arrows meeting one face: how far apart do they land",
  "source": "plan-proposal",
  "source_detail": "Q4 of how-a-graph-reads; bundle sizes and today's near-overlap count measured over the committed graphs",
  "explanation": "Pinning every forward arrow to one point per face would collapse 87 bundles of arrows onto 87 single points, where today only 2 pairs in 47 land close enough to touch — so some spacing rule is a repair, not a flourish. [The three answers that set a spacing by hand](#by-hand) differ in how much they spend and whether departures and arrivals look alike; [the answer that sets none](#measured) keeps what the code already derives from each arrow's own direction and merely folds it onto the chosen face. Leaves out the choice of face, which is settled.",
